---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../../../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Agricola Feeding Rules: How to Never Take a Begging Card"
  description="Two food per family member, every harvest. Here's how to plan for it instead of panicking."
  game="agricola"
  gameTitle="Agricola"
  category="Rules"
  product="rice-reed-tokens"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Every Agricola player remembers their first begging card. You've built a
    lovely farm, you've grown your family to four, the harvest arrives, and you
    discover you have three food and need eight. That's −15 points and a game
    that's effectively over.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Each harvest costs 2 food per adult and 1 per newborn. Work out your bill for the next harvest before you grow your family, not after — and build a cooking improvement early." />

  <h2>The Bill</h2>

  <p>
    At every harvest's feeding phase you pay:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>2 food</strong> for each adult family member</li>
    <li><strong>1 food</strong> for each family member born since the previous harvest</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Miss any of it and you take one begging card per food short, each worth
    <strong>−3 points</strong> at the end. There is no way to discard them.
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable title="What each harvest costs you">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Family size</th>
          <th>Food per harvest</th>
          <th>Across 6 harvests</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>2 (starting)</td>
          <td>4</td>
          <td>24</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>6</td>
          <td>36</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>4</td>
          <td>8</td>
          <td>48</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>5</td>
          <td>10</td>
          <td>60</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Harvests are not evenly spaced">
    The gaps between harvests shrink as the game goes on. Late in the game you can
    face two harvests within a few rounds of each other, which is exactly when your
    family is largest and your bill highest. Check the round track — the harvest
    rounds are marked, and knowing when the next one lands is free information
    most beginners never look at.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Raw Goods Are Not Food</h2>

  <p>
    This is the rule that ruins first games. Grain, vegetables, sheep, boar and
    cattle are all worth points at the end, but none of them feed anybody until
    you can <strong>convert</strong> them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Conversion needs an improvement — a Fireplace, a Cooking Hearth, or something
    similar. Without one, your only food sources are the handful of board actions
    that give food directly, and those are contested by everyone.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Build a cooking improvement early">
    A Fireplace in the first few rounds is one of the strongest opening moves in
    the game. It turns your animals and vegetables from decorative into edible and
    removes the food problem for the rest of the game. Cooking Hearths convert at
    better rates if you can get one.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Where Food Actually Comes From</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">BEST</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Grain, baked</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Sow a field, harvest grain over several turns, bake it into food with an oven or hearth. Slow to set up, but it feeds you passively for the whole game — it's the closest thing Agricola has to an engine.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">GOOD</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Animals, cooked</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Sheep and boar breed on their own if you keep two of a type with space to grow. Eating the surplus while keeping the breeding pair is efficient — but every animal eaten is points lost at the end, so it's a genuine trade.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">EMERGENCY</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Day labourer and direct food spaces</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Reliable, always available, and a waste of a precious action. Using these once is fine. Building your whole food plan around them means you'll never develop your farm.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The Rule That Prevents Every Disaster</h2>

  <p>
    Before you take the "grow your family" action, do this arithmetic:
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    How many harvests are left? What will my family size be? Can I actually
    generate that much food between now and then?
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Growing to four people two rounds before a harvest, with no cooking
    improvement and no grain in the ground, is how good positions collapse. The
    extra actions are worth a lot — but not −9 points.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Grow early or don't grow at all">
    A fifth family member added in round 12 gives you two or three extra actions
    and costs you 2 food at every remaining harvest. Family growth is powerful
    precisely because it compounds, and late growth doesn't compound. If you've
    missed the window, spend those actions on points instead.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>When a Begging Card Is Correct</h2>

  <p>
    Rarely, but it happens. If taking one begging card lets you complete a
    pasture, fill your last farmyard space and secure several categories, −3 can
    genuinely be the cheaper option.
  </p>

  <p>
    The key is that it must be a decision, made in advance, with the maths done.
    Nobody has ever accidentally stumbled into a good begging card.
  </p>

  <p>
    For how the harvest fits into the round structure, see
    <a href="/games/agricola/how-to-play-agricola/">the complete rules</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
